;; Generated background nebula (the modern FS1-style nebula a mission picks with +Nebula: and
;; +Color:). Mods replace this whole table with their own generated_nebula.tbl, or add to and
;; override it by name with *-gneb.tbm files.

#Generated Nebula Colors

;; +RGB: the color of the brightest parts; dimmer parts fade evenly toward black.
;; The built-in colors are FS1's: each FS1 mission palette held a 20-step ramp from black
;; to one color, and +RGB is that ramp's brightest entry.

$Name: Red
+RGB: 151 0 0

$Name: Blue
+RGB: 0 55 183

$Name: Gold
+RGB: 107 91 27

$Name: Purple
+RGB: 107 0 95

$Name: Maroon
+RGB: 107 0 39

$Name: Green
+RGB: 0 95 0

$Name: Grey blue
+RGB: 0 67 91

$Name: Violet
+RGB: 63 51 79

$Name: Grey Green
+RGB: 0 87 63

#End

#Generated Nebula Patterns

;; A pattern has two layers, combined so the clouds brighten the background without clipping:
;;   - a procedural background made from noise (+Density through +Seed), unless +Clouds Only
;;   - placed clouds (+Cloud), broken up by the same noise according to +Cloud Detail
;;
;; Fields, all optional, in this order:
;;   +Clouds Only:   YES for no procedural background, only the placed clouds
;;   +Cloud Detail:  how much the noise breaks up the clouds, 0 (smooth) to 1 (ragged)
;;   +Cloud:         lon lat width height angle brightness, repeated for each cloud
;;                   lon/lat: center in degrees (lon 0..360 wraps, lat -90..90)
;;                   width/height: size in degrees of longitude, across and along the cloud
;;                   (full width at half brightness); angle: rotation in degrees
;;                   brightness: peak, 0..1 (overlapping clouds add up, capped at 1)
;;   +Density:       background: fraction of the sky with bright patches
;;   +Frequency:     noise frequency around and along the sphere (unequal values give streaks)
;;   +Octaves:       noise detail layers; fewer gives broader, smoother masses
;;   +Warp:          how much the noise is swirled, stretching it into wisps
;;   +Contrast:      background falloff curve for the bright patches
;;   +Intensity:     overall brightness multiplier
;;   +Seed:          noise seed, giving each pattern its own variation
;;   +Resolution:    sphere mesh resolution (around, along)
;;   +Band:          range of the sphere covered, 0..1 from pole to pole
;;
;; The built-in patterns are clouds only, laid out to match the FS1 originals.

$Name: Nebula01
+Clouds Only: YES
+Cloud Detail: 0.35
+Cloud: 222.7 -14.5 70.8 11.0 164 0.73
+Cloud: 136.6 -48.5 41.1 8.7 4 0.55
+Cloud: 217.5 36.7 60.6 10.4 6 0.46
+Cloud: 255.5 -4.0 44.3 22.1 155 0.87
+Cloud: 291.1 4.2 56.7 9.3 2 0.53
+Cloud: 160.1 -2.3 36.2 18.5 176 0.75
+Cloud: 93.4 2.4 53.0 18.7 8 0.42
+Cloud: 313.9 -8.7 46.5 8.7 13 0.39
+Cloud: 288.0 42.2 33.6 12.4 175 0.54
+Cloud: 188.6 -13.5 37.5 8.7 159 0.53
+Cloud: 110.0 -9.7 48.1 13.5 167 0.72
+Cloud: 109.6 -47.1 22.5 11.3 14 0.35
+Cloud: 274.3 -23.7 35.8 17.3 13 0.26
+Cloud: 152.6 -42.3 9.1 10.7 126 0.27
+Cloud: 228.5 2.6 36.6 14.0 10 0.29
+Cloud: 153.5 -9.2 34.0 10.4 4 0.37
+Cloud: 83.0 -11.5 33.6 10.0 8 0.31
+Cloud: 163.0 -47.2 34.9 6.0 14 0.24
+Frequency: 5 6
+Warp: 0.40
+Seed: 1
+Resolution: 48 20

